// List, Bag, and CircularList Report
Creating a List

For this assignment, I created a List template class using a doubly linked list with head and tail pointers. The lecture version had incomplete copy move members, so I fixed the rule of five: copy constructor and copy assignment deep-copy the nodes, move constructor and move assignment just steal the pointers and leave the other list empty. I also added a self-assignment check and a size() method.

 Refactoring Bag to Use a List

I swapped Bag's backing store from std::vector to my List. insert() calls push_back(), and pop() calls back()/pop_back().

This works for Bag because it never needs to access items by position — just add, remove, count, or scan everything once, which a list handles fine. It would not work for something like ReceiptBag, since receipts are usually looked up by position, which needs a vector's constant-time operator[]. A linked list would have to walk from the front each time.

Building a CircularList

CircularList inherits from list. Traversal wraps from the tail back to the head instead of stopping, and takes a starting index so it knows when to stop (once it gets back to where it started). No index defaults to starting at the front, matching normal list traversal.

The only base class change needed was making Node, head, and tail protected instead of private, so Circular List could walk the nodes. Everything else (push/pop, copy/move) is inherited unchanged.